---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadLatest } from "../lib/data";
import { day, listAnd } from "../lib/format";
import { AUTHOR, REPO_URL } from "../lib/site";

const L = loadLatest();
const rows = Object.entries(L.sources);
const homes = L.districts.filter((d) => d.home).map((d) => d.name);
---
<Base title="About the data · Texas Ag Dashboard" description="Where the Texas Ag Dashboard gets its public data, how I summarize it, and how I make and score forecasts." current="about">
  <h1>About the data</h1>
  <p class="lede">The dashboard brings together public reports, summaries I calculate from those reports,
    and forecasts I make from public data.</p>

  <p class="notice"><strong>{L.nass_notice}</strong></p>

  <h2>How to read the figures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Published data.</strong> USDA’s statewide harvest progress and the U.S. Drought Monitor’s
      county drought figures come from those publishers. The seven-day weather forecast comes from the
      National Weather Service. Look for the source and date beside each figure.</li>
    <li><strong>Dashboard summaries.</strong> I combine published county drought figures to show
      conditions across each district and calculate comparisons such as rainfall versus normal.
      These summaries describe what the available data shows.</li>
    <li><strong>Dashboard forecasts.</strong> I label my predictions as outlooks and show when I issued
      them and which period they cover. The cotton harvest outlook estimates what a future USDA report
      will say for Texas as a whole.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="dashboard-forecasts">How I forecast cotton harvest progress</h2>
  <p>I use USDA’s weekly Texas cotton reports from earlier seasons to learn how harvest progress usually
    changes. The model looks at the time of year, how much of the crop USDA says is already harvested,
    and how fast harvest moved in the previous week. If that previous report is missing, I use the
    typical weekly gain for that time of year.</p>
  <p>I use the errors from testing the model on earlier seasons to set the estimated range. On the cotton card,
    <strong>Texas cotton forecast accuracy</strong> shows how far my forecasts missed on average,
    how often the USDA result fell in the estimated range, and how the forecasts compare with using
    the typical weekly gain alone. I save each prediction when I issue it and score it against the
    first USDA result I receive for that same week.</p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>I hide figures when they pass the age limit for their source.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">Sources and how current each one is</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Data date</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([, s]) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{s.url ? <a href={s.url}>{s.name}</a> : s.name}{s.license ? ` (${s.license})` : ""}</th>
            <td>{!s.as_of ? "none yet" : s.as_of.endsWith("-01-01") ? s.as_of.slice(0, 4) : day(s.as_of)}</td>
            <td><span class={`tag ${s.stale ? "ban" : s.delayed ? "" : "ok"}`}>{!s.as_of ? "Not yet available" : s.stale ? "Not current: hidden" : s.delayed ? "Stale: last good refresh" : s.between_reports ? "Between seasons" : "Current"}</span></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each source gives</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USDA NASS Quick Stats</strong>: weekly crop progress, crop condition and pasture condition for Texas
      as a whole. NASS doesn't publish these by district or county.</li>
    <li><strong>U.S. Drought Monitor</strong> (National Drought Mitigation Center, USDA, NOAA): the share of each
      county in each drought category, weekly. The statewide number is the Drought Monitor's own.</li>
    <li><strong>USDA AMS Market News</strong>: the Texas Weekly Cattle Auction Summary and individual auctions
      (Giddings, San Angelo). I show head-weighted average prices for Medium and Large No. 1 feeder cattle in
      100 lb weight classes, and all slaughter cows together. For San Angelo sheep and goats, I average the
      reported prices per hundredweight for slaughter hair lambs and slaughter goat kids in lots averaging
      50–69 lb, weighting by head count. The reporter's own summary is quoted as written.</li>
    <li><strong>Texas A&amp;M Forest Service</strong>: county burn bans, from the daily map behind their
      <a href="https://tfsweb.tamu.edu/wildfire-and-other-disasters/burn-bans-and-information/">burn ban page</a>.
      County judges and commissioners courts set bans; check yours before you burn.</li>
    <li><strong>USDA APHIS New World Screwworm</strong>. I count confirmed Texas animal cases and wild-fly trap
      detections from USDA’s public export. The source date is when I last verified the download. I flag the
      figures after three days without a successful refresh and hide them after 14 days. The USDA release
      map shows planned coverage. Texas Animal Health Commission publishes the movement restriction map.</li>
    <li><strong>Texas Boll Weevil Eradication Foundation</strong>. On the Cotton page I show trapping and
      treatment totals for the foundation’s three reporting areas. I hide the figures more than 14 days
      after the report’s week-beginning date.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://www.weather.gov/documentation/services-web-api">National Weather Service</a></strong>:
      the 7-day forecast high, low and rain at each Texas county's center. Public domain.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/nclimgrid-daily">NOAA NCEI nClimGrid-Daily</a></strong>:
      daily high, low and rain averaged over each county: the observed weather, and, from 1991–2020, the
      normals I compute (each calendar day's 30-year mean, smoothed over a month), so "vs normal" compares like with like.
      NOAA publishes about a week late, so weather figures say the day they run through, and the temperature
      chart marks the days since as not yet reported. An average over a county is a rough guide; your gauge
      will differ. Public domain.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://water.noaa.gov/resources/downloads/precip/stageIV/">NWS/NCEP Stage IV</a></strong>:
      daily rain estimated from radar and adjusted to rain gauges by the National Weather Service's River
      Forecast Centers, which I average over each county. It fills the days NOAA's county averages haven't
      reached yet and is always labeled an estimate. It often runs a little wetter than those averages, so it
      never goes into a "vs normal" figure, and NOAA's numbers replace it when they come out. Public domain.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Once a year, on the county cards and district pages:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USDA NASS Census of Agriculture</strong> (2022; next in 2027): farms and ranches, land in farms,
      farm size, sales and livestock for every county.</li>
    <li><strong>USDA NASS county estimates</strong>: planted acres and yield by crop, and cattle on Jan. 1. NASS
      publishes only some counties each year, so I show the newest published year for each, and district
      totals are a lower bound.</li>
    <li><strong>Rangeland Analysis Platform, USDA ARS</strong>: satellite estimates over rangeland only: plant cover
      by type each year since 1986, and forage production every 16 days, compared with the 10-year normal for the
      same date.</li>
    <li><strong>USDA NASS Cropland Data Layer</strong>: what's growing where, classified from satellite imagery at
      30 m, summed by county. County cards show up to six crop categories with the most acres in either the
      newest or earliest available year. The share is of all county land.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Featured districts</h2>
  <p>{`${listAnd(homes)} are the districts I started with, so they get links in the header and first place on the Texas page. Every one of Texas's ${L.districts.length} NASS districts has the same full district view, with a card for every county.`}</p>

  <h2>How I compute what isn't published</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Districts</strong> are NASS agricultural statistics districts. NASS stopped publishing
      district crop estimates after 2019. I calculate district figures from county data where available,
      such as total published crop acreage and the land-area-weighted share of each district in drought.</li>
    <li><strong>A county's drought category</strong> on the maps is the most severe category covering at least half
      of the county. The county card gives the shares.</li>
    <li><strong>"A year ago" and "5-year average"</strong> compare to the report nearest the same date in earlier
      years. I only show a 5-year average when all five years reported that week.</li>
    <li><strong>Out of season</strong>: crop condition ratings stop after harvest. Then I say so and give the last
      report date, instead of showing last season's numbers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Free, from public data</h2>
  <p>This site is free to use: no ads, no sign-up, no subscriptions, and nothing is sold here.
    Government agencies, universities, and public reporting programs publish the data I use.
    It is a personal project by {AUTHOR}, and the code is <a href={REPO_URL}>on GitHub</a>.</p>

  <h2>Not an official source</h2>
  <p>This is a free, plain-language read on public data. It is not
    endorsed by, or affiliated with, USDA, NASS, AMS, the Drought Monitor, NOAA, Texas A&amp;M or the State of Texas.
    Where I differ from them, they are right. Found a wrong number?
    Let me know by <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>opening an issue on GitHub</a>.</p>
  <p class="muted">{`Built ${day(L.generated_at.slice(0, 10))} from data as of ${day(L.asof)}.`}</p>
</Base>
